# AND THE OLD LOG NAMES THE WRONG TREE, which neither review could see and which I found writing this one. Its
# header takes the commit from a git rev-parse at LOG-WRITING time, not at archive time. The archives were
# taken when HEAD was 7d1a6fe; the header names a later commit. So a reader tying its POST tree to a blob
# would tie it to a tree that never ran. The old log keeps its bytes; this one supersedes it.
#
# WHY NOT A COMPANION. Both driver trees were deleted in scratch cleanup, so the old run's basis is not
# provable after the fact by any amount of prose. Re-running is the only honest fix.

